president Trump's pledged to invest a  trillion dollars in infrastructure made  no mention of our country's outdated  rail system and track is planning a  summer of urgently needed upgrades those  improvements will lead to more delays  and cancellations at the nation's  busiest train hub New York's Penn  Station Peter Greenberg is on a train  from Philadelphia to New York with why  our passenger rail service has struggled  for decades  Peter good morning good morning it might  be hard to believe but 2017 marks the  53rd birthday of the high-speed bullet  train service in Japan  Amtrak which was created 46 years ago  has struggled since it began service  decades of insufficient funding and  infrastructure investment have left  America's passenger rail network lagging  behind most of the developed world for  over a century railroads with a backbone  of America trains move people between  cities and provided Freight Transport  for industry but by the mid 20th century  competition from interstate highways and  Airlines forced many railroads out of  business in 1971 Amtrak was established  as America's passenger rail service but  from day one it was plagued with  problems it still hasn't solved most  routes never made a profit and in many  cases Amtrak doesn't even own the tracks  there's about a hundred and fifty trains  on the whole Northeast Corridor Stephen  Gardner overseas planning for Amtrak in  order to run fast frequent reliable  passenger trains you really need to  build a system for for just that a  dedicated a dedicated system but it's  not just dedicated systems its funding  new tracks across the entire Amtrak  network the Acela which is supposedly  our high-speed rail system can only go  150 miles per hour on a small section of  track  to compare the bullet train in Japan can  reach speeds of 200 miles an hour to  make the 250 mile trip between Tokyo and  Osaka  we're decades behind our counterparts  from around the world Deborah Hersman is  president and CEO of the National Safety  Council part of this is because we don't  have a dedicated funding stream that  supports that initiative the good news  is that Amtrak has ordered 28 high-speed  trains for the Northeast Corridor with  the first of these scheduled to be in  service by 2021 the problem there are  currently no plans or the necessary  funds to build the tracks needed to  support high-speed rail  I think what's lagged is kind of the  broader issue which is investment in  infrastructure and transportation  generally that if you look across our  network we are under investing in all of  our assets those dedicated systems are  now being built some with private money  in California a high-speed rail link  between the Los Angeles area and San  Francisco is now under construction but  we'll need to be patient it won't be  finished before 2029 at the earliest but  in South Florida the nation's first  privately owned higher speed rail  project bright line is moving faster and  set for launch later this year in  Florida when bright line gets up and  running it will handle the two hundred  and five mile run between Miami and  Orlando with speeds of up to 125 miles  an hour now that's still technically not  high speed but at least while bright  line may be new it's not solving  Amtrak's problems and the continuing  need to move people by rail we have to  rebuild the infrastructure to make sure  that it stands the test of time and can  operate decades into the future our  economy is riding on the back of this  infrastructure and we've got to take  care of it  in the end it all gets down to money  Amtrak goes to more than 500  destinations across America but if the  current Trump budget plan is approved  many Amtrak officials are worried they  may have to close more than 200 stations  across America Alex Peter thanks  important story about Amtrak and  infrastructure
 

